From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 20:36:41 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] media: rkvdec: map the RCB SRAM through the DMA API

The RCB buffers come out of an SRAM gen_pool, and the decoder reaches
them through the same IOMMU as everything else, so they have to be
mapped into its address space. That mapping is made by hand: iommu_map()
is called on rkvdec->iommu_global_domain with the SRAM's kernel virtual
address as the IOVA, and the registers are then programmed with that
same value.

The domain is the wrong one to hand-map into. It comes from
iommu_get_domain_for_dev(), which is the device's DMA domain -- the one
whose IOVA allocator serves every dma_alloc_coherent() the driver makes
for frame buffers. Nothing tells that allocator the RCB range is taken,
so it will hand the range out again. On RK3588 the allocator works down
from the top of the 4 GiB aperture, and once a workload has about 2 GiB
of buffers mapped it reaches the RCB, where rk_iommu_map_iova() finds a
valid PTE and returns -EADDRINUSE:

  iova: 0x0000000081178000 already mapped to 0x00000000ff079000 cannot
  remap to phys: ... prot: 0x3
  rkvdec-core fdc38000.video-codec: dma alloc of size 49766400 failed

0xff079000 is vdec1_sram's base. The IOVA is not a random address
either: __iommu_map() does not check the aperture and rockchip-iommu
masks an IOVA with 0xffc00000 / 0x003ff000 and casts to u32, so the
64-bit ioremap address aliases silently to its low 32 bits. Neither the
size of the failing allocation nor the bit depth of the stream has
anything to do with it -- three concurrent 8-bit 8K decodes collide at
the same address.

dma_map_resource() is the interface for giving a device a physical range
that is not RAM. It takes an IOVA from the same allocator the frame
buffers use, so the range cannot be handed out twice, and it needs no
knowledge of which domain is in play.

Signed-off-by: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 .../platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec-rcb.c     | 33 ++++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+), 11 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec-rcb.c b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec-rcb.c
index 977e37cf2..8d15e2c00 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec-rcb.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec-rcb.c
@@ -9,8 +9,9 @@
 #include "rkvdec.h"
 #include "rkvdec-rcb.h"
 
-#include <linux/iommu.h>
+#include <linux/dma-mapping.h>
 #include <linux/genalloc.h>
+#include <linux/iommu.h>
 #include <linux/sizes.h>
 #include <linux/types.h>
 
@@ -77,7 +78,9 @@ void rkvdec_free_rcb(struct rkvdec_dev *rkvdec, struct rkvdec_core *core)
 			virt_addr = (unsigned long)cfg->rcb_bufs[i].cpu;
 
 			if (rkvdec->iommu_global_domain)
-				iommu_unmap(rkvdec->iommu_global_domain, virt_addr, rcb_size);
+				dma_unmap_resource(rkvdec->main_core->dev,
+						   cfg->rcb_bufs[i].dma, rcb_size,
+						   DMA_BIDIRECTIONAL, 0);
 			gen_pool_free(core->sram_pool, virt_addr, rcb_size);
 			break;
 		case RKVDEC_ALLOC_DMA:
@@ -142,14 +145,22 @@ int rkvdec_allocate_rcb(struct rkvdec_dev *rkvdec, struct rkvdec_core *core,
 							SZ_4K);
 		}
 
-		/* If an IOMMU is used, map the SRAM address through it */
+		/*
+		 * If an IOMMU is used, the SRAM has to be mapped into the
+		 * decoder's address space.  Go through the DMA API rather than
+		 * calling iommu_map() directly: the domain here is the device's
+		 * DMA domain, so an address chosen by hand is one the IOVA
+		 * allocator does not know is taken and will hand out again to a
+		 * frame buffer, and rockchip-iommu truncates an IOVA to 32 bits
+		 * without complaint, so the collision need not even be visible
+		 * in the address that was picked.
+		 */
 		if (cpu && rkvdec->iommu_global_domain) {
-			unsigned long virt_addr = (unsigned long)cpu;
-			phys_addr_t phys_addr = dma;
+			dma_addr_t iova = dma_map_resource(rkvdec->main_core->dev,
+							   dma, rcb_size,
+							   DMA_BIDIRECTIONAL, 0);
 
-			ret = iommu_map(rkvdec->iommu_global_domain, virt_addr, phys_addr,
-					rcb_size, IOMMU_READ | IOMMU_WRITE, 0);
-			if (ret) {
+			if (dma_mapping_error(rkvdec->main_core->dev, iova)) {
 				gen_pool_free(core->sram_pool,
 					      (unsigned long)cpu,
 					      rcb_size);
@@ -158,10 +169,10 @@ int rkvdec_allocate_rcb(struct rkvdec_dev *rkvdec, struct rkvdec_core *core,
 			}
 
 			/*
-			 * The registers will be configured with the virtual
-			 * address so that it goes through the IOMMU
+			 * The registers are configured with the mapped address
+			 * so that the access goes through the IOMMU.
 			 */
-			dma = virt_addr;
+			dma = iova;
 		}
 
 ram_fallback:
